WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
Mass curfews, mass quarantines

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:03.840
lockdowns everywhere.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.600
But let’s talk about mass surveillance.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.080
Want to fight COVID-19?

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:14.240
There’s an app for that.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.800
Facial recognition, GPS, Bluetooth.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:17.920
They make it easy.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.360
Cameras and drones keep an eye on us too.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.760
This is a kind of watershed event.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.560
Surveillance is really spreading everywhere.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.000
It’s all about tracking people to contain the spread.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.560
But some want to know: Who’s policing the police?

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.000
So how will our governments protect our health

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.320
and our privacy?

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.080
And how do we make sure all this extra surveillance

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.840
doesn’t stick around longer than we need it?

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.680
This coronavirus has been tough to contain

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.880
because it spreads so easily.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.200
It’s why governments are trying to do two things:

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.480
monitor people’s health and keep tabs on where they are.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:06.400
And surveillance technology makes that a lot easier.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.320
Singapore’s government released this app

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.520
that logs everyone you bump into.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.320
The German government has an optional smartwatch app

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:17.520
that tracks your pulse, temperature and sleep.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.880
And if you’re quarantined in Hong Kong

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:21.960
you have to wear an electronic wristband.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.560
Police are also flying drones.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.280
This US company says it can even tell if you’re sick.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.560
Anonymised data on social distancing, heart rate

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.800
respiratory rate and fever detection.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:37.560
That’s a lot of technology to

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.040
help stop the spread of COVID-19.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.280
And privacy advocates have noticed.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.200
I’m not against surveillance itself.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.320
We need to make use of

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:51.840
whatever technology is available to fight the epidemic

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.360
and to ease the economic crisis.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.560
But it should be done carefully.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.200
Technology has to be used and data gathered in a way

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.280
that’s necessary and proportionate

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.040
to a particular end.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.880
Thing is — even before the pandemic

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.560
we were giving away a lot of our data already.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:12.760
We share personal information online all the time.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:14.040
Even the stuff we don’t post

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.360
like what we google and what we buy.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.040
We give our fingerprints to governments when we travel

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:22.120
or to our smartphone to unlock the screen.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.800
Companies like Apple and Samsung

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:25.760
have a record of our faces.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.880
Intelligence services use facial recognition too.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.800
And all of that data is being collected

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.040
sometimes with, sometimes without

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:35.200
our consent.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.080
Police in the UK have been building up

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:40.920
a facial recognition database

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.240
by scanning people in designated areas.

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:45.520
Last year they used the tech to track down

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.600
more than 450 suspects in Wales.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.080
We don’t know how long it’s being stored

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.160
on that database and for what purpose.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.920
Russian police also use facial recognition

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.600
to monitor protesters.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.880
With biometric data collection seemingly available

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.600
at nearly every street corner the authorities say

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.440
they can easily catch criminals.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.000
Other countries like India and Kenya

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.960
are phasing in a national ID card

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:13.280
that’s linked to your biometric data.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:16.200
Possibly the world’s most privacy invasive

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.360
digital ID system — lots of very sensitive data

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.800
about people, so including their iris scans

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:24.400
their earlobe shape, their GPS.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.120
Without the card people can’t access things

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:28.760
like loans or healthcare.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:30.880
And now we have a pandemic.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:33.000
Governments everywhere are expanding their power

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.720
to bring this global threat under control.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:37.840
In China they’ve harnessed one of the most

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.640
pervasive surveillance systems in the world.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.360
Cameras are pretty much everywhere

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.600
one for roughly every eight people.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.320
Police once used facial recognition to track down

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:54.240
a wanted man at a concert with 60,000 people.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.560
Cameras are even used to enforce

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:59.800
China’s social credit system which ranks businesses

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.920
and citizens on their behavior.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:04.760
Back in the ’40s and ’50s that was all done through

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:07.320
humans asking their neighbours lots of questions.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.720
Now that’s really all gone high tech.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:13.280
And because of COVID-19 it’s gone into overdrive.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:17.880
In Hubei, where the virus first broke out

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:20.520
police used cameras, drones and facial recognition

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:23.160
to quarantine 56 million people.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.920
Even as lockdowns ease up millions of Chinese people

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:28.520
are still being monitored.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:31.160
In some cities they have to log their health

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:34.920
and travel history in an app that generates a colour code.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:36.520
To leave home or go shopping

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:39.360
security has to give you the green light.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.560
If you’re red or yellow you’re really not meant

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:43.160
to be moving around.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:44.520
It’s not just China.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:46.960
Surveillance is being stepped up everywhere.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:49.000
So how far is too far?

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.040
Yuval Harari, a historian and author

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:53.680
says it could open us up to abuse.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:57.280
It is, of course, focused on the disease itself.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.640
The system needs data about what's happening

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:03.720
inside your body — your body temperature

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:06.760
maybe your blood pressure, maybe your heart rate.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:10.920
But once surveillance goes under the skin

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:15.120
it can start to be used for many, many other purposes.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:17.960
They can know not only that you're watching

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:20.640
but also what you’re feeling.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:23.400
What makes you angry? What makes you laugh?

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:28.480
It gives some clue about your political views or personality.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:33.000
And the implications of that are extreme.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:34.600
It can go all the way to the

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.840
establishment of new totalitarian regimes

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.480
worse than anything we've seen before.

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:43.040
That’s obviously a worst-case scenario.

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:45.160
But what’s to stop that from happening?

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:46.440
Well that’s the key question, right?

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:49.160
That’s why Amnesty International

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:53.600
as well as other NGOs and digital rights organisations

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:57.080
are very closely tracking the rollout of these systems.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:59.440
Privacy advocates say we should scrutinise

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:00.440
the emergency laws

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:02.240
governments are passing right now.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:04.760
Often with little or no public debate.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:08.160
The UK, Hungary, Russia, the Philippines, Thailand

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:09.160
they've all done it.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:10.600
Israel tried too.

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:14.720
The government has turned to the secret police

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:19.360
and authorised it to use some of their skills and technologies

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:22.360
it initially developed to monitor

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:23.880
and control the Palestinians.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.640
But the Israeli Supreme Court put on the brakes.

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:31.760
We know, especially in Israel, that measures taken

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:35.560
in an emergency have this nasty tendency

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:38.200
to long outlast the emergency.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:40.120
We need to be extremely careful

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:42.000
about what we're allowing and for how long.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.120
Will we be able to turn that back once this is over?

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:46.640
And then there’s the language

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:48.640
some politicians have been using.

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:51.960
I view it as, in a sense, a wartime president.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:55.440
We must act like any wartime government.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:57.320
We are at war.

00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:00.120
There are plenty of examples where conflict has led

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.200
to governments expanding their powers.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:11.840
9/11 is a good example.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:28.200
The Patriot Act was brought in just 45 days later

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:30.440
in the name of fighting terrorism.

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:31.840
And it was criticised for expanding

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:33.680
US government surveillance.

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:36.160
And some of those powers are still around.

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:40.200
Human rights are born out of the modern world’s turmoil.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:42.760
Now is precisely the time where we need to

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.320
protect them and uphold them.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:46.960
So how do we make sure the authorities don’t abuse

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:48.520
the data they have on us

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:50.240
in the name of fighting the pandemic?

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:54.320
This is not a war. This is a healthcare crisis.

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:56.800
If you want to put somebody in charge

00:07:56.800 --> 00:08:00.680
put a nurse in charge — not a soldier or a general.

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:02.720
Some actually think governments in Asia

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:04.000
are doing it right.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:06.120
In Singapore and South Korea, for instance

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:08.680
officials say the data they’re collecting now

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:10.000
will be deleted later.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:13.520
There’s also been in some cases a lot of public data

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:16.080
and public transparency around why they’re

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:18.040
doing things and how they’re doing things

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:20.400
such that people understand.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:23.120
It’s not just necessarily a kind of power grab.

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:27.400
Citizens should demand that first of all

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:30.200
their privacy be protected as far as possible

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:34.080
and that any increase in monitoring of the citizens

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:36.520
be accompanied by an increase in

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:38.240
the monitoring of the government.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:39.880
So maybe that’s it.

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:41.840
Sure, surveillance technology will help

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:43.520
contain the pandemic.

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:45.480
But while they’re watching us

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:48.600
we might want to be watching them.

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:53.280
“We don’t know when we’ll be back on

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:55.560
the Start Here set but not to worry.

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:57.840
We’re still going to be bringing you more episodes

00:08:57.840 --> 00:08:59.040
any way we can.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:01.400
In the meantime our website aljazeera.com

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:04.360
has all the latest on the coronavirus pandemic.

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:05.000
I’ll see you next week.”

